SM(212,{"n":"1 Timothy 2","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Timothy 2.mp3","d":2965.1,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.16,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[22.0,"Chapter 2, verse 1."],[25.36,"Therefore. So we've talked before about how St."],[31.86,"Paul uses this word, therefore, as a transition."],[35.24,"He lays out some truth."],[37.61,"And then he says, Therefore, we go to application."],[40.59,"So he's just laid out what we just said."],[42.91,"Here's the goal."],[44.69,"Here are some particular groups that we need to work with on this goal."],[47.73,"And so when he says, therefore now, he's going to go to the real practical."],[51.01,"Therefore, here's what you need to do, Timothy."],[56.62,"I exhort first of all that supplications, prayers, intercessions, and giving of thanks be made for all men."],[66.3,"For kings and all who are in authority, that we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and reverence."],[77.82,"Step okay. Say Timothy, step one."],[81.68,"We need to offer prayers."],[83.52,"We need to offer prayers for all men."],[86.89,"And that includes kings and all who are in authority."],[95.13,"Um interesting thing about the fact that he uses the word kings here."],[100.33,"There was no king of Ephesus"],[106.13,"There were no kings anywhere near Ephesus."],[108.63,"We're in the Roman Empire."],[110.95,"And the Roman Emperor did not consider himself a king."],[115.19,"In fact, Rex was sort of a dirty word to the Romans."],[120.55,"Because they associated the word king with the literally the tyrants."],[125.21,"The rulers who had ruled before the republic."],[127.53,"So the one title that the Roman Emperor never took was king."],[131.45,"And would never would never use for himself."],[133.77,"Emperor comes from imperator, which basically means conqueror."],[137.87,"Um, so it's okay to be a conqueror, but you didn't want to call yours call yourself a king."],[143.03,"So when he says k,ings here, uh St."],[147.15,"Paul is choosing his words deliberately."],[149.23,"Because he he could have said it's not like you would get in trouble for saying offer prayers and intercessions for the emperor."],[155.33,"Like no one would get mad at that."],[157.46,"Uh, so why does he say kings?"],[159.86,"Well, the way that the Romans hated the title king is the way most Jewish people felt about the Roman Emperor."],[169.06,"Especially since, as we talked about last time when this would have been written, Nero was the Roman Emperor."],[176.58,"So, not even a very good one by any standard."],[180.18,"There's actually an exhibit right now in Europe where they're trying to rehabilitate Nero."],[183.94,"It's like good good luck with that."],[185.86,"Good luck with that."],[190.7,"So, uh so when he says kings, he's saying that to mean when I say everyone, I mean everyone."],[201.26,"The people persecuting the church."],[203.26,"We need to make prayers, intercessions, and give thanks for."],[209.6,"And all the authorities, the authorities at Ephesus."],[212.58,"When I say everyone, I mean everyone."],[215.54,"We need to make prayers and intercessions for."],[218.42,"How does that follow from what he just said?"],[220.1,"Well, he just named just about everybody."],[222.6,"And it's because we desire everybody to be saved."],[225.82,"For everyone to find salvation."],[228.22,"Step one, we pray for everybody that that would happen."],[236.18,"And notice it was so that we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and reverence."],[247.95,"In reference to the rulers."],[249.97,"Meaning we don't pray that God would, you know, strike them with worms like Herod, get rid of them."],[258.27,"We're praying for their health and well being."],[261.49,"So that we could all live peaceably."],[265.81,"Because there were a lot of Jewish folks in particular at this point who were praying for revolution."],[273.43,"They were getting ready to have one that was going to result in the destruction of the temple at this time."],[282.83,"Verse 3, for this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Savior, who desires all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth."],[293.63,"So we, the reason we want all to be saved and we pray for everyone is that God and our Lord Jesus Christ want all men to be saved and come to the knowledge of the truth."],[309.99,"And St. Paul isn't like innovating here."],[312.31,"This isn't like a new idea."],[314.73,"This is all through the Old Testament."],[317.21,"God takes no delight in the death of a wicked man, but that he should turn and live."],[321.23,"Over and over again."],[322.43,"This is always who God has been."],[324.93,"And so we need to have that same attitude."],[328.05,"And since that is God's attitude, then when we pray for that, we're joining our will and our desire and our prayers to what God is doing in the world, to God's will."],[341.27,"And that in turn will change us."],[346.39,"Verse 5 for there is one God and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus, who gave himself a ransom for all to be testified in due time, for which I was appointed a preacher and an apostle."],[362.59,"I am speaking the truth in Christ and not lying, a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and truth."],[370.51,"So he says there's one God, the Father."],[375.91,"There's one mediator between God and man, Christ."],[381.43,"And as we've talked about before, I won't go too far down this rabbit trail now, but as we've talked about before, um, the uh this mediator idea is not just like Jesus is a man and he prays for us to God."],[403.05,"And no one else does."],[404.59,"Or he's the only priest or something."],[405.07,"s Which is how this is sometimes quoted by some of our friend."],[410.47,"Um the idea is he's already identified."],[415.53,"See, we take we take verses and little snatches and we pull them out of context."],[421.67,"How many times has St."],[424.79,"Paul already called Christ Lord?"],[429.27,"Several in a couple paragraphs."],[432.56,"Kyrios, which is the way that the name Yahweh is transliterated in the Greek of the Old Testament."],[440.9,"He's already identified Christ as God several times."],[443.94,"So when he identifies him as man here, he's talking about mediator in exactly the sense the church would later talk about it."],[451.14,"That Christ is both God and man."],[453.7,"And that is how he mediates."],[478.42,"That already happened historically."],[481.36,"To be testified to in due time."],[483.6,"Not everybody knows about it yet."],[486.53,"Not everyone in the world knows about it."],[488.81,"But in due time they will."],[492.3,"Because, amongst others, that's why St."],[495.5,"Paul was called to be a preacher and an apostle."],[498.7,"And a teacher, remember, Gentiles means nations and a teacher of the nations."],[503.58,"He says, This is the job I was called to do, to go and testify to that fact of who Christ is and what he did."],[509.82,"That's the gospel."],[511.02,"Who Christ is and what he did."],[513.42,"St. Paul's going to make sure everybody finds out about it."],[518.78,"Okay. Now, the ransom for all."],[522.3,"This is a point people get hung up on."],[524.91,"And part of the problem we have interpreting these New Testament passages is there there are are some New Testament passages like this where it says all."],[534.99,"Well, all that everybody."],[535.79,"Christ offered himself for everybody"],[538.77,"And then there's other passages that seem to teach something different."],[543.59,"Like when Christ in St."],[545.11,"Matthew's Gospels says he's going to say to some people, be gone, I never knew you."],[550.33,"Well, that doesn't sound like he offered himself for them."],[553.55,"That seems to conflict."],[556.31,"And this is because we've talked about this before, but this is because uh there are multiple elements, to our to our salvation."],[567.85,"Now, this says he offered himself as a ransom for all."],[571.37,"And as we've talked about before, when that ransom language and that redemption language is used, redemption is the same kind of language."],[579.57,"Think about redemption the way you redeem a coupon."],[584.63,"Or you redeem a notice."],[588.01,"It's the same kind of idea as ransom, as."],[591.07,"You make some sort of payment and receive something in return that has otherwise been held."],[599.03,"That redemption language and that ransom language is all tied up with Christ's victory over death in particular."],[607.99,"So Christ's victory and what Christ did, who's victorious over sin, death, the devil."],[618.63,"Those are three different things."],[620.39,"So St Paul has already clearly taught, we just went through this in his epistles to the Thessalonians, talking about the resurrection."],[630.11,"Christ has redeemed everyone who ever lived from death."],[638.11,"On the last day, everyone is going to rise from death."],[644.27,"Death holds no one."],[651.63,"Death doesn't have them."],[652.95,"Because Christ has redeemed them from death."],[654.11,"ed them from death. He's ransom"],[658.35,"Death is sometimes personified, but he's not a guy who Christ paid money to."],[662.61,"But we understand."],[664.06,"Um that's everybody."],[666.87,"And that's why when you look at the icon of the resurrection, you see Christ grabbing Adam and Eve."],[675.51,"First parents of everybody."],[677.61,"All humanity is saved from death."],[680.01,"Now you notice you don't see him in that icon grabbing Cain."],[687.45,"Or Judas. You see King David and King Josiah, you don't see like Ahab and Jezebel."],[697.08,"You see the prophets and the saints."],[700.04,"Because redemption from sin is not universal in the New Testament."],[705.96,"Redemption from sin, as St."],[708.88,"Paul has already said here in 1 Timothy, is universally possible."],[715.04,"Repentance is available to anyone."],[717.6,"That was St."],[718.32,"Paul's whole point about him being the chief of sinners."],[720.9,"You could be the most far gone in the world."],[723.22,"It's possible for you to repent and receive salvation."],[726.66,"The thief on the cross, the last second you're alive, you can repent."],[731.26,"So repentance is a possibility for everyone."],[736.94,"But it is not automatically applied to everyone."],[743.8,"And then, of course, the Christ's defeat of the devil, his defeat of the demonic powers, that applies to ever.ybody as well, because you know they're defeated"],[753.33,"And so even the most the most wicked sinner on earth is still now living under the dominion of Christ and is going to be judged by Christ on the last day, who is merciful, not by demons."],[769.83,"So that also benefits everyone."],[773.83,"But repentance and forgiveness of sins is part of what St."],[777.43,"Paul is preaching."],[780.95,"Paul comes and he proclaims the gospel, who Christ is and what he's done, which is redeem everyone from death, which is defeat the spiritual powers of evil."],[794.19,"And then what do we see in the book of Acts when Saint Paul or any of the other apostles get done preaching?"],[799.95,"The people ask, What must I do to be saved?"],[803.87,"Okay, if that's true, then what do I need to do?"],[806.27,"And what is it?"],[807.31,"Repent and be baptized."],[811.07,"For the forgiveness of your sins."],[814.67,"And then the Christian life, then, is one of repentance."],[820.43,"So that's why he can say Christ gave himself as a ransom for all men."],[827.31,"Applies to everyone."],[831.31,"And that's what he's getting at."],[833.23,"So a last note. to our Calvinist friends"],[840.71,"Who I am so mean to, but uh, so our Calvinist friends, including some very prominent Calist teachers and apologists, like to pluck out this paragraph of 1 Timothy, chapter 2."],[865.19,"This little bit of chapter 2 we just read."],[867.67,"They don't start with chapter 1."],[869.27,"They don't walk you through chapter 1 and then go into chapter 2."],[872.31,"It's just let's pull out chapter 2."],[875.27,"And here's what they try to do."],[877.59,"Uh they try to say, uh, well, look."],[882.71,"Uh 1 Timothy 2, verse 1, there says, uh, giving of thanks be made for all men"],[888.99,"But then look, in verse 2, he says, for kings and all who are in authority."],[894.17,"Those are kinds of men."],[899.05,"So what St."],[900.25,"what Paul really means, they say, is we should pray for all kinds of men."],[907.13,"For all kinds of men."],[908.81,"Even these evil Roman rulers."],[911.29,"Why do they do that?"],[912.65,"Well, because then when they get to verse 3, and it says that he desires all men to be saved."],[918.65,"Well, it doesn't mean God desires all men to be saved."],[922.01,"It means he desires all kinds of men to be saved."],[922.65,"ires all kinds of men to be saved. Des"],[931.98,"So, and then he gave himself as a ransom for all, and then we're just gonna add the words kinds of men, even though they're not there."],[943.04,"So to a Calvinist, the saved include all types of people."],[950.0,"But yeah. So the elect are from all different types of people."],[954.8,"God chooses all different types of people to save, and then all different types of people descend to hell, and who can't be saved."],[961.46,"Um, so not only do you have to add a lot of words here that aren't actually here, and notice verse 2 there doesn't even list kinds of men."],[970.58,"even. It just says sort of even for these evil people"],[978.14,"It doesn't list a bunch of kinds."],[980.06,"St. Paul could have done that."],[981.34,"He says for kings and rulers and workers and slaves and men and women and Jews, Greeks, and right?"],[988.14,"St. Paul could have done that."],[989.34,"He lists types of people sometimes in his epistles."],[992.38,"Doesn't do that here, noticeably."],[994.7,"Um, but also again, we just walked through chapter one."],[1000.86,"What was he talking about in chapter one?"],[1004.86,"Salvation is available for everyone."],[1006.74,"For everyone. He's saying if it if it was available for a miserable sinner like me, it's available for these people I've excommunicated and thrown out of the church."],[1014.86,"out of the church. Cut them"],[1015.86,"He doesn't say they're reprobates who can't be saved."],[1022.34,"He doesn't say that the people causing trouble about the genealogies are clearly not elect."],[1028.18,"And that's why they're doing this."],[1031.6,"He says the exact opposite."],[1033.84,"He says salvation from sin is available to even the worst of sinners."],[1038.64,"And then he says, therefore."],[1042.02,"And goes right into this."],[1045.83,"So you have to take this passage radically out of context and then add a bunch of words to it to try and make it say what they want it to say."],[1055.19,"Whereas if you just follow through St."],[1058.07,"Paul's argument, starting with chapter 1, verse 1, through to here, it's very clear what St."],[1062.59,"Paul is saying."],[1065.28,"That salvation from death and from the fallen spiritual powers is a reality for everyone."],[1071.44,"And we're gonna keep seeing this as we keep going, too."],[1074.48,"But so it's not just what comes before, it's what comes after, also."],[1078.48,"You know, it should affect how we read this."],[1080.32,"But um, and that repentance and forgiveness of sins is available to everyone."],[1091.01,"Uh so yeah, that was my sorry Calvinists moment for for this evening."],[1100.53,"Yes, sir. Okay, good, death, not Calvinists."],[1102.37,"They've had enough"],[1103.45,"Okay. We need to lay off them."],[1109.37,"They've had enough."],[1111.77,"Don't pile on the Calvinists."],[1113.61,"But uh does this mean that prior to or apart from leave time out?"],[1121.61,"Yeah, apart from uh from Christ's action people would have died in the sense of ceasing to exist or died in the sense of being in an unpleasant Hades, what what yeah, Hades or Sheol, the realm of the dead."],[1143.05,"And that was the understanding of the ancient world"],[1149.33,"Yeah, that's that's sort of universal in in the in the ancient world."],[1152.77,"Yeah. That I used to teach with the students is when you're dead, you're dead, that's it, and it makes you miserable."],[1159.25,"Yeah. Yeah. It stinks."],[1161.33,"Yeah. Yeah. And how how it stinks varies a little."],[1165.33,"Like if it's Sheol in the ancient Near East, it's like really bad."],[1168.53,"It's like a slasher movie."],[1170.37,"Uh if if you're Greek, it's just sort of more emo, and every day is like Sunday, so silent and gray."],[1178.69,"And you know, it's like you're living in Manchester, England."],[1182.53,"Uh it's just sort of meh glum, you know, until you sort of fade away, you know."],[1190.85,"Um, ye yeah,ah."],[1191.65,"ah. Ye Yeah."],[1192.25,"So it's that kind of thing."],[1192.29,"But it's it's Hades, it's the realm of the dead"],[1193.13,"It's not hell like demons and pitchforks."],[1199.85,"Dust and ashes, you know, everything."],[1202.51,"Nor is it really non existence."],[1206.03,"No. No. I I think that's what most modern people think of death as being this non-existence."],[1214.03,"Yeah, yeah. And that was not an idea."],[1217.12,"The obviously the life that was in this body is now gone from the body, and so the body is now falling apart."],[1224.08,"Um, but that life went someplace."],[1228.25,"Down into Shoel."],[1229.63,"Um, and this is this is expressed in different ways when you look at like Second Temple Jewish literature."],[1235.91,"Generally, there's an idea that there's there's a part of Hades that's pretty much like that, that's like what the pagans think it is, and then there's a part that's like a little nicer."],[1248.87,"So like you read the book of Enoch, you read the book of Enoch or 1 Enoch, and there's these three caves where they are."],[1258.44,"And uh one of the caves where like the fathers from the Old Testament are has a spring of water in it."],[1266.62,"So it's not quite so bad."],[1269.0,"Um or um, well, I mean, you get in uh the parable of the rich man and Lazarus."],[1277.33,"The rich man is in the crummy, is in the crummy part, and you know, and Lazarus is with Abraham in the not as crummy part."],[1287.43,"I mean, it doesn't say there that like they're in paradise and they're everything's beautiful, it's just he's with Abraham."],[1296.57,"Yeah, yeah. And he's and so he's he's being comforted there."],[1299.77,"And so this is what gets picked up, like with our iconography of the harrowing of hell."],[1305.07,"Which is really the harrowing of Hades."],[1306.93,"The herrowing of Hades, is Christ going to that place and getting everyone and bringing them to paradise."],[1315.12,"And there actually is iconography, you don't see it as often, but there is iconography of all those people going into paradise."],[1320.48,"par There's this depiction of you seeadise, and it looks like the Garden of Eden."],[1320.56,"adise. Par And you usually see a couple of the patriarchs there."],[1330.98,"You see a bunch of little depictions of people who died as infants."],[1335.6,"There. And you see Saint Dismas, who is the thief on the cross, who Christ said, today you'll be with me in paradise, he's there."],[1342.64,"And then Christ is leading this big troop of the righteous from the old testament to now come in, also."],[1351.7,"Um, so yeah, that's the idea."],[1354.14,"Is that there was part of Christ's Christ's resurrection was this transition, for them."],[1362.23,"And that, of course, is an analogy."],[1364.37,"Time obviously works differently if you're not in a body and all those things."],[1368.58,"But you you can see the imagery there."],[1372.56,"Yeah, yeah. Um we tend to we apply time and space."],[1377.52,"I mean, we can say all we want that heaven isn't a place and hell isn't a place, but every time you think about it, you're thinking about a place."],[1384.1,"Because we're just we're humans and we're finite, and that's how it's same thing with time."],[1388.74,"We could say, yeah, well, there's no time there, but then when we imagine it, we still have time, you know."],[1395.06,"Um because of who we are."],[1397.38,"So yeah, yeah, that's the idea."],[1404.74,"So verse eight."],[1407.26,"I desire therefore that the men pray everywhere, lifting up holy hands without wrath and doubting."],[1417.9,"Okay, so we see already here the typical prayer position that we still use of lifting up hands."],[1423.5,"We see that everywhere in the ancient world."],[1427.9,"The idea is that they're praying without wrath and doubting."],[1432.23,"Without wrath. Who is he just talking about praying for?"],[1436.01,"Nero. So we're praying for these people who have been excommunicated."],[1441.31,"We're praying, the troublemaker, we're praying for everyone, and we're not praying with anger."],[1447.33,"We're not praying for a successful revolution against the Romans."],[1450.33,"We're not praying they'd all fall over and die."],[1453.74,"And without doubting."],[1455.19,"And doubting here is not like we think tend to think of doubting in terms of uh like atheism, like you doubt God exists or something, or you doubt."],[1465.13,"There were no atheists around here."],[1468.41,"So this doubting means sort of wavering."],[1471.55,"Like not really meaning it."],[1473.8,"Insincere. Verse nine, in like manner also that the women adorn themselves in modest apparel with propriety and moderation, not with braided hair or gold or pearls or costly clothing, but which is proper for women professing. godliness with good works"],[1494.98,"So he says, men need to be men of prayer."],[1500.12,"He's saying they need to be the ones doing this prayer I was just talking about."],[1503.96,"They need to be interceding."],[1505.77,"So every every man by virtue of being male has a certain priestly role."],[1511.87,"Every single one."],[1512.59,"And we'll talk about that when he starts talking about presbyters and deacons and bishops and deaconesses."],[1518.75,"He talks about deaconesses too."],[1520.67,"Um in a couple chapters here, not tonight, but um but men have a certain priestly role to intercede, before God."],[1531.57,"Um that he's describing here."],[1534.05,"And then he's saying women also notice in like manner."],[1543.22,"So remember, before this, in the Jerusalem temple, there was a court of women."],[1553.46,"Women could only come in so close."],[1558.16,"Men could come a little closer."],[1560.4,"Men who are priests could come a little closer."],[1563.62,"During specific times and with the washings and with all the things."],[1567.44,"So St. Paul is not saying that the women are now identical and there's no difference."],[1576.06,"He's not just saying people all do this."],[1576.86,"There's still a difference"],[1579.59,"But like manner means parallel, similar."],[1583.61,"Different, but in the same category."],[1587.71,"Why is that important?"],[1588.91,"We talked about this a little bit in First Corinthians."],[1593.04,"Uh part of the reason that he had to get into issues about men and women and worship in 1 Corinthians is that for your average person, well, for your average Jewish person, uh, they just thought women weren't as smart as men, and so wouldn't be able to understand the preaching and stuff."],[1613.78,"It wouldn't be able to understand any of this stuff."],[1616.1,"So they should just, you know, do whatever, go stand in the back and gossip and you know, talk about your girl stuff."],[1623.91,"And that's men will be over here, you know, studying the Torah."],[1627.64,"Um, so and that was right in the temple, there's literally a wall enforcing that."],[1637.38,"There's literally literally a wall keep it keeping women further out."],[1641.8,"So the idea that women are also, this is gonna be developed here in a second, that women are also called to be disciples."],[1651.9,"Which means learner or student, essentially, are also called to be disciples."],[1656.83,"This is part of that in like manner."],[1658.91,"So there's a change here now."],[1660.35,"No, don't send them."],[1662.95,"No, they need to be here too."],[1664.39,"They need to be listening, they need to be hearing, they need to be learning."],[1669.8,"Um and what he says to them, is well, we'll get to the other part in a second, but what he says right here to them is they don't need to, their goal when they're coming to the church."],[1684.39,"I know when you're going out in public, as a lady, you want to get your herd did."],[1691.01,"You want to get all your makeup right."],[1693.18,"You want to look right here."],[1694.54,"He's saying that's not what you should be concerned with."],[1698.92,"So he uses this sort of metaphor."],[1700.48,"Rather than adorning yourself by doing your hair and wearing fancy clothes and wearing all this jewelry to show off, you need to adorn yourself with good works."],[1712.02,"You need to adorn yourself with good works."],[1714.34,"So he's not presenting women as being sort of uninvolved, standing in the back."],[1720.66,"If the women are working, to help the community."],[1725.66,"Women are busy about things."],[1728.31,"In St. Paul's vision."],[1729.83,"The men are praying and interceding."],[1732.33,"And have this kind of priestly role, and the women are working and doing these good works and helping each other and supporting each other and supporting the community."],[1741.71,"So there's different tasks, but they're not like one above the other."],[1748.89,"Or women excluded somehow, parallel but different."],[1753.61,"Okay. Now we'll get to the controversial verse."],[1761.69,"Let a woman learn in silence with all submission."],[1765.85,"And I do not permit a woman to teach or to have authority over a man, but to be in silence."],[1773.37,"We'll go on in a second."],[1774.97,"So that's it gets worse before it gets better."],[1778.33,"No. So uh when it says learn in silence."],[1785.39,"This is one of those things where where things hit us differently in the modern world."],[1790.43,"We hear that, we say he's telling women to shut up"],[1796.23,"But learn in silence as opposed to what?"],[1801.35,"What's the opposite of learning in silence?"],[1805.59,"Not learning and talking."],[1807.35,"Yeah. Remember what I just said about going and gossiping at the back because you can't learn anything?"],[1816.15,"So he's saying women need to learn."],[1819.91,"Meaning they need to listen."],[1821.83,"They need to hear the teaching."],[1825.29,"And the word for silence there is actually the word that later gets used in the church for like meditation and meditative prayer."],[1832.57,"So St Paul is actually making a major change here in terms of embracing women rather than excluding women."],[1845.27,"In the Jewish context, women were not related to the covenant or to God directly."],[1854.76,"They couldn't be circumcised."],[1858.44,"A woman was an Israelite because her father or her husband was circumcised and was an Israelite."],[1867.18,"So that's what made you."],[1869.88,"Whatever the head of the household was, that's what the household was."],[1874.86,"Whereas St. Paul is giving women this responsibility as people."],[1883.27,"As he's saying, as a woman, you can't just be like, oh, you know, he'll take care of all this religious stuff."],[1888.71,"I'll just, you know."],[1891.8,"They need to come to know God."],[1895.08,"They need to come to know Christ."],[1897.72,"They need to repent of their sins."],[1900.36,"Not just have their husband go and repent for them."],[1903.72,"And repent for the family."],[1905.5,"They also. And that means they can't be standing in the back gossiping and talking."],[1911.52,"They need to be listening and learning and meditating and thinkinging. and practic silence"],[1918.82,"Because they have the same responsibility men do."],[1928.78,"So now we're gonna keep going and we'll come back to the uh the authority uh issue."],[1935.66,"Um well no, we'll go ahead."],[1938.06,"So it says the opposite."],[1939.84,"He ties having authority to teaching."],[1944.69,"So he's saying he doesn't want women teaching in the community, specifically in this case in Ephesus."],[1952.66,"Why might that be?"],[1956.58,"Well, it would scandalize the rest of society."],[1957.7,"It would scandalize the rest of Yeah in part."],[1963.1,"Who who did St."],[1964.46,"Paul run into trouble with when he was at Ephesus?"],[1967.5,"Do you remember?"],[1969.1,"The idol makers."],[1971.66,"The idol makers for Artemis."],[1977.6,"For Artemis. So the Artemis cult there had certain authority structures."],[1986.11,"Had certain authority structures."],[1988.59,"And St. Paul doesn't want those authority structures to be brought over into the church."],[1995.77,"Why? Well, because these pagan women may know things about magic."],[2005.78,"About paganism, pagan religion."],[2009.83,"But they need to learn about Christ."],[2015.2,"They need to learn about Christ."],[2017.76,"So that's the distinction that Saint Paul is making here."],[2022.1,"He's contrasting learning and teaching."],[2025.86,"He's saying they need to learn, because by the way, I believe they can."],[2031.06,"Pagans had an even worse view of women."],[2033.14,"You know, Aristotle said that women were a birth defect."],[2037.7,"He literally says that."],[2038.82,"He says every fetus is trying to become male, and some of them sort of come out half baked and they're women."],[2045.34,"So pagans, you know, but um, and so he's he's he's shifting this sort of in two ways."],[2055.82,"So this also goes with the way he talked about women dressing."],[2061.82,"Because these former pagans in Ephesus are all used to worshiping a woman."],[2068.86,"Were the authorities in there cult women?"],[2072.3,"Well, depends on what you mean by authority."],[2075.58,"The line between priestess in Greco-Roman paganism and temple prostitute was a thin one."],[2082.03,"But they're used to worshiping a woman."],[2086.2,"And so Saint Paul is doing two things here"],[2089.6,"Women aren't an object of worship."],[2095.25,"Women are not to be approached sexually."],[2099.17,"This is part of why he's talking about the modest attire."],[2103.63,"But then on the positive side, women are capable of becoming disciples of Christ and learning, meditating, repenting of their own sins."],[2112.37,"Developing a relationship with Christ and with God directly."],[2117.03,"And are responsible to do that."],[2119.36,"So he's getting it both pieces."],[2122.24,"Okay. Now. it's not over yet"],[2131.96,"For Adam was formed first, then Eve."],[2135.8,"And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived fell into transgression."],[2145.0,"Um he says originally."],[2152.46,"Adam is created first and then Eve."],[2155.26,"Why is he bringing up Adam?"],[2158.78,"What was Adam created to do?"],[2164.22,"Well, to serve as a priest."],[2166.22,"You went straight to king, but also priest."],[2169.0,"Because those two went together."],[2171.29,"So Adam was supposed to be serving as a priest in the temple that was paradise."],[2180.47,"And part of that meant what in relationship to Eve."],[2187.51,"He was supposed to be, bringing her to God as well."],[2192.42,"He was supposed to be kidding."],[2193.7,"If he's serving as a priest, first of all, in terms of his own family, meaning his wife, and then eventually his children, he's going to be praying for her, interceding for her, teaching her about God."],[2205.16,"Since he was formed first."],[2206.52,"He was around first."],[2208.02,"So God revealed himself to him first."],[2210.18,"Teaching, bringing her along to come to know God."],[2212.84,"And teaching her and what happened."],[2213.8,"Well, the exact opposite"],[2220.72,"Now, when you read closely the story in Genesis 3."],[2226.42,"It says Eve has this conversation with the serpent with the devil."],[2231.44,"And she eats the fruit and then offers some to her husband."],[2234.96,"What does that tell you?"],[2237.12,"He was standing right there the whole time."],[2244.64,"If he's a priest, what is he supposed to be doing?"],[2248.88,"He's supposed to be protecting the sanctity of this sacred place."],[2252.18,"Like driving the devil out of it when he shows up and starts saying this stuff."],[2256.94,"Protecting his wife."],[2260.7,"Correcting what the devil is saying and saying, No, God did say we're not to eat of this tree."],[2265.26,"Don't do it."],[2266.3,"Not sitting there and watching her do it and seeing what happens."],[2275.39,"So when he talks about Adam and Eve here, he's not saying, see, look, women were no good from the beginning."],[2284.39,"He's saying, no."],[2287.19,"If men fail to take this role they're supposed to take, the one he called on them to take at the beginning of this passage."],[2295.65,"Then women won't have this role they're supposed to take of learning and coming to know God."],[2301.91,"And being protected."],[2303.43,"And the whole thing breaks down when you invert the way things are supposed to be."],[2309.03,"And both fail, and they end up getting cast out."],[2311.59,"And that's not what we want."],[2312.71,"That's bad. So, and we'll see this as we continue to."],[2319.56,"We're gonna end here at the end of chapter two in a minute, but uh we'll see this as we continue on."],[2324.92,"That uh, but I'll I'll leave this as a as a marker."],[2328.44,"Uh, St. Paul is not defending conventional Roman or Jewish hierarchies in 1 Timothy."],[2336.12,"He just isn't, and we're gonna see that become very clear as we go on."],[2341.16,"But it's even it's even true here, in the sense that he's saying, number one, women are equally responsible to come to know Christ and to repent and to work out their salvation as men are, which is already a change from Judaism."],[2356.8,"From the preceding Judaic belief, and a huge difference from paganism."],[2362.85,"He's also saying that the pagan reality in Ephesus that they're used to is the same kind of inversion, the same kind of demonic inversion that you see happen in Genesis 3."],[2374.61,"Where they've propped up this feminine form of this woman and adorned it, as a as a goddess."],[2383.2,"And are worshipping it."],[2384.66,"And that results in destruction, that results in sin."],[2385.78,"You invert it"],[2387.26,"So we can't, we don't do that."],[2390.75,"And for that to happen, for things to function properly."],[2393.65,"Men need to fulfill their role, not as the boss."],[2398.38,"Not as the authority figure, like we were talking about with the title father."],[2402.99,"But they need to fulfill their role as what?"],[2406.03,"Priests, teachers. They need to fulfill that role."],[2410.85,"And then women need to take on this huge new responsibility that they didn't have culturally before."],[2418.15,"To learn rather than to teach."],[2421.49,"And and to give and to come to know Christ and to work out their own salvation."],[2425.82,"So this this is is what St."],[2426.3,"We got one more verse"],[2427.7,"Paul is talking about here."],[2433.14,"Which is badly translated into the New King James."],[2435.78,"But hey, this is what we got to work for."],[2438.98,"Verse 15 Nevertheless, she will be saved in childbearing if they continue in faith, love, and holiness with self control."],[2450.42,"So, uh, here's a couple problems."],[2456.18,"Uh notice how it shifts from singular to plural in this translation."],[2462.26,"She will be saved in childbearing if they continue in faith, love, and holiness with self control."],[2470.54,"Of course, that would be faithfulness, love, holiness with self-control."],[2475.82,"Who's they? Her and the kid?"],[2480.86,"Her and her husband?"],[2482.06,"Just everybody? It doesn't make any sense."],[2483.52,"It's a bad translation."],[2484.56,"Okay. So what it actually says is for meaning because."],[2493.76,"Because woman will be saved by childbearing."],[2498.88,"By childbirth. If they continue."],[2505.01,"So woman is singular."],[2506.61,"That's why they throw in a she."],[2508.95,"But they means what?"],[2512.07,"Actual women. So you have woman in the singular."],[2517.33,"And the word woman in the singular is not the word for a woman."],[2522.7,"Like you probably know in in, well, you may know."],[2525.02,"In Greek, we've talked about this before."],[2526.86,"That there's a word anthropos that means man, like humanity."],[2531.84,"Like mankind. And then there's another word anir that means like a man."],[2537.68,"Like this guy."],[2539.66,"There's the same thing with women."],[2542.22,"There's uh yeni, that means a woman or a wife."],[2546.14,"That's a root from which we get gynecologist."],[2548.68,"And there's yineki, which means like woman, like womanhood, like woman."],[2556.42,"Which also, by the way, was Eve's original name, was Isha, woman."],[2564.02,"So he's so woman, we're talking about Eve now."],[2568.32,"This concept of womanhood, woman writ large, in the same way that Adam's name means man."],[2574.4,"Woman is saved by childbearing if they continue."],[2581.46,"So we have two different subjects of two different verbs."],[2585.62,"This is also important."],[2586.98,"Because some people read this and want to say, well, this means women get saved by having babies."],[2595.12,"But we have two different subjects of two different verbs."],[2600.74,"So woman, woman writ large, womanhood is saved through childbearing."],[2610.62,"Just talked about the first Eve, how this all gets inverted, goes bad."],[2617.74,"What do you hear?"],[2619.1,"The woman gave birth to a child as a Christian."],[2622.78,"What do you think?"],[2626.06,"Theotokos. That's what the tokos means."],[2629.66,"Childbearing. Bearing a child."],[2633.88,"This is part of where St."],[2635.16,"Irenaeus in the middle of the second century gets the whole theology of the Theotokos being the second Eve."],[2643.76,"The second woman."],[2646.94,"There's a bunch of other places too."],[2648.3,"Like there's a verse of Jeremiah that says, I tell you a mystery, now the woman shall encompass the man."],[2654.06,"Woman came out of man with Eve, and then the woman encompasses the man, meaning Christ is in her womb."],[2660.88,"Christ is the second Adam that St."],[2662.88,"Paul talked about in Romans 5."],[2665.14,"So woman is saved by a childbearing."],[2667.54,"Eve is not like the last word on womanhood anymore."],[2673.78,"The Theotokos is."],[2675.94,"So the childbearing in question is the childbearing of the Theotokos."],[2680.43,"The birth of Christ."],[2682.16,"Because the subject, the subject of that verb is woman."],[2684.08,"Womanhood Eve. That's the subject of that verb."],[2695.52,"So womanhood, femininity, if you will."],[2699.25,"Is saved by childbearing, is transformed by childbearing."],[2703.73,"That's the last word now."],[2705.17,"One specific childbearing."],[2706.43,"So we have this contrast between the first Eve and the second Eve."],[2712.28,"So if what happens, St."],[2714.12,"Paul is saying in Eden was an inversion of the way things were supposed to be, then the relationship between the Theotokos and Christ is an inversion of that inversion, which puts things right."],[2727.82,"Two one eighties is a 360."],[2730.94,"So it sets things right."],[2732.62,"And so then the second verb, if they continue, that's now plural."],[2738.38,"So this is now talking about women."],[2740.78,"Not woman writ large, but women."],[2744.99,"Therefore, women are saved if they continue in faithfulness, love, and holiness with self control."],[2757.12,"Why does St."],[2758.24,"Paul have to point this out?"],[2764.05,"We could go."],[2765.41,"I won't go back there and read it to you."],[2768.09,"Because it's kind of gross."],[2769.69,"But there's a passage in Ben Sirac where Ben Sirac makes a very similar argument to this, with a different conclusion."],[2778.65,"Talking about how Eve sinned first."],[2782.33,"That C, women are evil from the beginning."],[2786.73,"He explicitly makes that argument."],[2793.81,"Paul is like quoting Ben Sirac here or directly referencing Ben Sirach."],[2798.37,"But Ben Sirach certainly represents a strand of the Pharisaic tradition that St."],[2804.61,"Paul would have heard."],[2807.57,"And so Saint Paul is pointing to the Theotokos again to correct that."],[2811.81,"correct. To that by saying Eve isn't the last word"],[2818.15,"There's nothing evil about woman or women."],[2822.57,"There's nothing defective about women, you pagans."],[2826.31,"But, they too, they find salvation."],[2828.98,"That that womanhood itself was redeemed."],[2830.82,"There's this transformation of the relationship between man and woman that happens in the relationship between the Theotokos and Christ."],[2839.16,"And the way that plays out is that salvation is now available to women, which is why they need to learn and develop this relationship personally now."],[2849.98,"It's not just about the men doing better than Adam."],[2853.56,"Christ did better than Adam."],[2855.0,"Christ is the second Adam, completes what Adam failed to do."],[2858.68,"And so now men, in emulation of Christ, find their salvation."],[2863.5,"In part by fulfilling this priestly role that Adam failed to fulfill."],[2869.74,"Women likewise find their salvation in Christ because the Theotokos has transformed that relationship."],[2875.58,"And so now, they can by continuing in faithfulness, love, holiness, self-control, which are the same things he's always telling men to have."],[2886.03,"In like manner, remember."],[2887.57,"It's not a completely different thing."],[2888.93,"Men find salvation this way and women do this way."],[2891.57,"It's in like manner."],[2892.77,"The way that expresses itself, is that by them fulfilling these two different parallel roles?"],[2905.07,"So all that said, completely non-controversial."],[2910.27,"Um actually the controversial part is gonna be when people hear hear this recording and they haven't heard the rest yet."],[2917.87,"That whole thing I said about him not uh endorsing traditional hierarchies, that's where people are gonna flip out on Reddit."],[2924.51,"You watch, uh, but then they'll see what I'm talking about when we go on."],[2928.03,"Uh, because I'm gonna be talking about slaves, for example."],[2931.55,"But anyway, um, we will pick up next time uh at the beginning of chapter three."],[2939.15,"So thank you, everybody."],[2942.71,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2950.07,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2955.43,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":22.0,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:1-2 - Therefore: pray for all","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Therefore\"</b> = Paul's transition from truth to <b>application</b></li><li class=\"d1\">just laid out the <b>goal</b> and the <b>groups</b> to work with on it</li><li class=\"d1\">now the \"real practical\": \"here's what you need to do, Timothy\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1-2</b>: supplications, prayers, intercessions, thanksgiving for <b>all men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"for <b>kings</b> and all in authority\" - so we lead a <b>quiet and peaceable life</b> in godliness and reverence</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Step one</b>: offer prayers for <b>all men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">includes kings and all who are in authority</li><li class=\"d0\">Notable that he says <b>\"kings\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no king of Ephesus, none anywhere near it - we're in the <b>Roman Empire</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Emperor did not consider himself a king</li></ul>"},{"t":95.13,"lv":3,"h":"Why 'kings'? Rex as a dirty word","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Rex</i> = \"sort of a dirty word to the Romans\"</li><li class=\"d1\">associated king with the <b>tyrants</b> who ruled before the republic</li><li class=\"d1\">the one title the Emperor <b>never took</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Emperor</b> from <i>imperator</i> = basically <b>conqueror</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul is choosing words <b>deliberately</b></li><li class=\"d1\">praying for \"the emperor\" would get no one in trouble - \"no one would get mad at that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so why \"kings\"?</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans hated the title king; most <b>Jewish people</b> felt that way about the <b>Emperor</b></li><li class=\"d1\">especially as <b>Nero</b> was Emperor when this was written - \"not even a very good one by any standard\"</li><li class=\"d1\">exhibit in Europe trying to rehabilitate Nero - \"good luck with that\"</li></ul>"},{"t":190.7,"lv":3,"h":"'Everyone means everyone'","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"When I say <b>everyone</b>, I mean <b>everyone</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">the people <b>persecuting the church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the authorities in <b>Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How does it follow? He just named just about everybody</li><li class=\"d1\">because we <b>desire everybody to be saved</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Step one</b>: we pray for everybody, that this would happen</li><li class=\"d0\">Purpose: <b>quiet and peaceable life</b>, in reference to the rulers</li><li class=\"d1\">not praying God would \"strike them with <b>worms</b> like <b>Herod</b>\" / get rid of them</li><li class=\"d1\">praying for their <b>health and well-being</b> so all can live peaceably</li><li class=\"d0\">Many Jewish folks were praying for <b>revolution</b></li><li class=\"d1\">getting ready to have one → <b>destruction of the temple</b></li></ul>"},{"t":282.83,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:3-4 - God desires all to be saved","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:3-4</b>: good and acceptable to <b>God our Savior</b>, who desires <b>all men</b> to be saved and come to the <b>knowledge of the truth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reason we pray for everyone = <b>God and our Lord Jesus Christ</b> want all saved</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>not innovating</b> - \"not a new idea\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all through the OT: God takes <b>no delight in the death of a wicked man</b>, but that he turn and live</li><li class=\"d1\">\"over and over again. This is always who God has been\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So we need the <b>same attitude</b></li><li class=\"d1\">praying for that = <b>joining our will, desire and prayers to what God is doing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"that in turn will <b>change us</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":346.39,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:5-7 - One God, one mediator","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:5-7</b>: one God, <b>one mediator</b> between God and men, the <b>man Christ Jesus</b>, gave himself a <b>ransom for all</b>, testified in due time; Paul appointed <b>preacher, apostle, teacher of the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">One God = the <b>Father</b>; one mediator = <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mediator</b> is not just \"Jesus is a man and prays for us to God, and no one else does\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"he's the only priest\" - how some of \"our friends\" quote it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>out of context</b>: \"we pull little snatches out\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Already identified in context</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul has called Christ <b>Lord</b> several times in a couple paragraphs</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Kyrios</i> = how <b>Yahweh</b> is transliterated in the Greek OT</li><li class=\"d1\">already identified Christ as <b>God</b> several times</li><li class=\"d0\">So \"man\" here = mediator in the sense the church later spoke of</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ is <b>both God and man</b> - \"that is how he mediates\"</li></ul>"},{"t":453.7,"lv":3,"h":"Ransom for all, testified in due time","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ransom for all</b> = everybody</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Testified in due time</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>already offered himself</b> - happened historically</li><li class=\"d1\">not everyone in the world knows yet - \"in due time they will\"</li><li class=\"d0\">That's why Paul called as <b>preacher, apostle, teacher</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gentiles</b> = nations → teacher of the nations</li><li class=\"d1\">his job: go and testify to <b>who Christ is and what he did</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"That's the <b>gospel</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul will \"make sure everybody finds out\"</li></ul>"},{"t":518.78,"lv":2,"h":"Ransom for all: the apparent conflict","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Ransom for all\" - a point people get <b>hung up</b> on</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem interpreting NT passages that say <b>\"all\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some say Christ offered himself for <b>everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others seem different: Matthew - \"<b>be gone, I never knew you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't sound like he offered himself for them\" → seems to conflict</li><li class=\"d0\">Answer: <b>multiple elements</b> to our salvation</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ransom / redemption</b> language</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>redeeming a coupon</b> - you make a payment, receive what was held</li><li class=\"d1\">tied up with Christ's <b>victory over death</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ victorious over <b>three</b> different things</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sin</b>, <b>death</b>, the <b>devil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul already taught it in <b>Thessalonians</b> (resurrection)</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ has <b>redeemed everyone who ever lived from death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">last day: everyone rises - \"death holds no one\"</li></ul>"},{"t":700.04,"lv":3,"h":"Redemption from sin: possible, not automatic","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Language around <b>Pascha</b>: \"nobody's left in the tombs\"</li><li class=\"d1\">death sometimes personified, but not a guy \"Christ paid money to\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Icon of the resurrection</b>: Christ grabbing <b>Adam and Eve</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first parents of everybody → all humanity saved from <b>death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but not grabbing <b>Cain</b> or <b>Judas</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Redemption from sin</b> is <b>not universal</b> in the NT</li><li class=\"d1\">as in 1 Timothy: <b>universally possible</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>repentance</b> available to anyone</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul \"<b>chief of sinners</b>\" - most far gone can repent</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>thief on the cross</b> - last second you're alive</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not automatically applied to everyone\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Defeat of the devil</b> applies to everybody too</li><li class=\"d1\">even the most wicked sinner lives under <b>Christ's dominion</b></li><li class=\"d1\">judged on the last day by Christ, \"who is <b>merciful</b>, not by demons\"</li></ul>"},{"t":773.83,"lv":3,"h":"Preaching, Acts, and the life of repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Repentance and forgiveness</b> are part of what Paul preaches</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul proclaims the gospel: <b>who Christ is</b>, what he's done</li><li class=\"d1\">redeemed everyone from <b>death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">defeated the <b>spiritual powers of evil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In <b>Acts</b>, after any apostle preaches: \"<b>what must I do to be saved?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">answer: <b>repent and be baptized</b> for the forgiveness of sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Christian life = a life of <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So he can say Christ gave himself as ransom for <b>all men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"applies to everyone\"</li></ul>"},{"t":833.23,"lv":2,"h":"A note to our Calvinist friends","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Last note to our <b>Calvinist</b> friends - who I am so mean to\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Prominent Calvinist teachers <b>pluck out</b> this paragraph of <b>1 Tim 2</b></li><li class=\"d1\">don't start with chapter 1 and walk through</li><li class=\"d1\">\"just, let's pull out chapter 2\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Their move:</li><li class=\"d1\">2:1 \"all men\" - then 2:2 \"kings and all in authority\" = <b>kinds of men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so Paul means pray for <b>all kinds of men</b>, even evil Roman rulers</li><li class=\"d1\">then v.3 \"desires all men saved\" = <b>all kinds of men</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then \"ransom for all\" - \"add the words <b>kinds of men</b>, even though they're not there\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Result: saved include all types; <b>elect</b> from all types; all types also descend to hell</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem 1: <b>adding a lot of words</b> that aren't there</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem 2: v.2 doesn't list kinds at all</li><li class=\"d1\">just \"even evil people\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>could have</b> listed kings, workers, slaves, men, women, Jews, Greeks - does so elsewhere</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't do that here, noticeably\"</li></ul>"},{"t":994.7,"lv":3,"h":"Chapter 1 context says the opposite","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We <b>just walked through chapter 1</b>: salvation available to <b>everyone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"if it was available for a miserable sinner like me, it's available for these people I've <b>excommunicated</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul does <b>not</b> say they're <b>reprobates</b> who can't be saved</li><li class=\"d1\">\"cut them out of the church\" - not \"can't be saved\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not that the <b>genealogies</b> troublemakers are \"clearly not elect\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"says the <b>exact opposite</b>\": salvation from sin available to even the worst</li><li class=\"d0\">Then \"<b>therefore</b>\" - right into this passage</li><li class=\"d0\">Calvinist reading: <b>radically out of context</b> + add a bunch of words</li><li class=\"d0\">Follow the argument from <b>1:1</b> → clear: salvation from <b>death</b> and the <b>fallen spiritual powers</b> real for everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">repentance and forgiveness available to everyone</li><li class=\"d0\">Also what comes <b>after</b> affects how we read it - \"keep seeing this\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Sorry Calvinists moment</b> for this evening\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1100.53,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: what is death before Christ?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Aside: \"don't pile on the Calvinists\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: apart from Christ's action, would people have <b>ceased to exist</b>, or been in <b>Hades / Sheol</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>Hades or Sheol</b>, the <b>realm of the dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">understanding of the <b>ancient world</b> - sort of universal</li><li class=\"d1\">\"when you're dead, you're dead... makes you miserable\"</li><li class=\"d0\">How it stinks <b>varies</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ancient Near East <b>Sheol</b>: \"really bad... like a <b>slasher movie</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greek</b>: \"more emo\", every day like Sunday, <b>silent and gray</b> - \"living in Manchester, England\"; \"glum\" until you fade away</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not hell like demons and pitchforks\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dust and ashes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>non-existence</b> either</li><li class=\"d1\">modern idea of death as non-existence \"was not an idea\" then</li><li class=\"d1\">life leaves the body, body falls apart, but <b>life went someplace</b> - down into Sheol</li></ul>"},{"t":1229.63,"lv":3,"h":"Second Temple views: Enoch, Lazarus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Second Temple Jewish literature</b>: Hades has <b>parts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a part like what pagans think</li><li class=\"d1\">a part \"a little nicer\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Enoch</b>: <b>three caves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one where the <b>fathers from the OT</b> are has a <b>spring of water</b> - \"not quite so bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rich man and Lazarus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rich man in the \"<b>crummy part</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Lazarus with <b>Abraham</b> in the \"not as crummy part\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't say paradise, everything beautiful - just <b>with Abraham</b>, <b>comforted</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1299.77,"lv":3,"h":"Harrowing of Hades iconography","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Basis of our iconography of the <b>harrowing of Hades</b> (\"harrowing of hell\" - really <b>Hades</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ goes to that place, gets everyone, brings them to <b>paradise</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Less common icon: people <b>entering paradise</b></li><li class=\"d1\">looks like the <b>Garden of Eden</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a couple <b>patriarchs</b>; little depictions of people who <b>died as infants</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Dismas</b>, the thief - \"today you'll be with me in paradise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ leading a big troop of the <b>righteous of the OT</b> in</li><li class=\"d0\">Part of Christ's <b>resurrection</b> = a <b>transition</b> for them</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>time works differently</b> if not in a body</li><li class=\"d0\">We apply <b>time and space</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"heaven isn't a place, hell isn't a place\" - but we always imagine a place</li><li class=\"d1\">same with time - \"because we're humans and we're finite\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1404.74,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:8 - Men pray without wrath","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:8</b>: men pray <b>everywhere</b>, <b>lifting up holy hands</b>, without <b>wrath and doubting</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Typical <b>prayer position</b> we still use - lifted hands</li><li class=\"d1\">seen <b>everywhere</b> in the ancient world</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Without wrath</b>: who was he just talking about praying for?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Nero</b>; the excommunicated; the troublemaker - everyone</li><li class=\"d1\">not praying with <b>anger</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not praying for a <b>successful revolution</b> against Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">not praying they'd \"all fall over and die\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Without doubting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not atheism - \"no atheists around here\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>wavering</b>, \"not really meaning it\" - <b>insincere</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1473.8,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:9-10 - Women, in like manner","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:9</b>: women adorn in <b>modest apparel</b>, propriety, moderation; not braided hair, gold, pearls, costly clothing - but <b>good works</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Men = <b>men of prayer</b>, the ones <b>interceding</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"every man by virtue of being male has a certain <b>priestly role</b>\" - every single one</li><li class=\"d1\">will come back to this with <b>presbyters, deacons, bishops</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he talks about <b>deaconesses</b> too - \"a couple chapters here, not tonight\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Men's role: <b>intercede before God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then \"women <b>also</b>\" - notice <b>\"in like manner\"</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1543.22,"lv":3,"h":"'In like manner': court of women","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jerusalem temple: <b>court of women</b></li><li class=\"d1\">women only so close, men a little closer, priests closer still</li><li class=\"d1\">specific times, washings</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>not</b> saying women now identical, no difference</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<i>in like manner</i> = <b>parallel</b>, similar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"different, but in the <b>same category</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why important? Same issue as <b>1 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">average Jewish person thought women <b>not as smart</b>, couldn't understand preaching</li><li class=\"d1\">\"go stand in the back and gossip\"; men over here studying <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in the temple, \"literally a <b>wall</b>\" keeping women further out</li><li class=\"d0\">Change: women also called to be <b>disciples</b> = <i>learner, student</i></li><li class=\"d1\">\"no, don't send them... they need to be here too\"</li><li class=\"d1\">need to <b>listen, hear, learn</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1669.8,"lv":3,"h":"Adorn with good works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Going out in public: \"get your hair did\", makeup right</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>not</b> what you should be concerned with</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Metaphor</b>: not adorning with hair, fancy clothes, jewelry to show off</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>adorn yourself with good works</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not women \"uninvolved, standing in the back\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's vision: the community has</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>men</b> praying and interceding - priestly role</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>women</b> working, doing good works, helping and supporting each other and the community</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Different tasks, but not one <b>above</b> the other\"</li><li class=\"d1\">women not <b>excluded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Parallel but different</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1753.61,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:11-12 - Learn in silence","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Now the <b>controversial verse</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:11-12</b>: woman learn in <b>silence</b>, all submission; no teaching or authority over a man</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Learn in silence\" - modern ears: \"<b>telling women to shut up</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">opposite of learning in silence? \"<b>Not learning and talking</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">recall: gossiping at the back \"because you can't learn anything\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So: women need to <b>learn</b> = <b>listen</b>, hear the teaching</li><li class=\"d0\">Word for <b>silence</b> = later used in the church for <b>meditation, meditative prayer</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul making a <b>major change</b>: <b>embracing</b> women, not excluding</li></ul>"},{"t":1845.27,"lv":3,"h":"Women and the covenant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jewish context: women <b>not related to the covenant or to God directly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't be <b>circumcised</b></li><li class=\"d1\">an Israelite because <b>father or husband</b> was circumcised</li><li class=\"d1\">\"whatever the <b>head of the household</b> was, that's what the household was\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul gives women <b>responsibility as people</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't say \"he'll take care of all this religious stuff\"</li><li class=\"d1\">must come to <b>know God</b>, know <b>Christ</b>, <b>repent of their sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not have husband \"repent for the family\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't be standing in the back gossiping</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>listening, learning, meditating, thinking, practicing silence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"same responsibility men do\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1928.78,"lv":2,"h":"Teaching and authority; Artemis at Ephesus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Will come back to the <b>authority</b> issue</li><li class=\"d0\">He <b>ties authority to teaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't want women teaching in the community - <b>specifically at Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why? Would <b>scandalize</b> society - in part</li><li class=\"d0\">Who did Paul have trouble with in Ephesus? <b>Idol makers for Artemis</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Artemis cult</b> had certain <b>authority structures</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul doesn't want them <b>brought over into the church</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan women may know about <b>magic</b>, paganism, pagan religion</li><li class=\"d1\">but they need to <b>learn about Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Distinction: <b>learning vs. teaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"they need to learn, because by the way, I believe they <b>can</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2033.14,"lv":3,"h":"Pagan views; two moves by Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pagans had an <b>even worse view</b> of women</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle</b>: women a <b>birth defect</b> - literally</li><li class=\"d1\">every fetus trying to become male; some \"come out half baked\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul shifting it <b>two ways</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ties to women's <b>dress</b>: former pagans in Ephesus used to <b>worshiping a woman</b></li><li class=\"d1\">line between <b>priestess</b> and <b>temple prostitute</b> in Greco-Roman paganism \"a thin one\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Negative</b>: women <b>not objects of worship</b>; not approached sexually</li><li class=\"d1\">part of why he talks modest attire</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Positive</b>: women can become <b>disciples of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">learn, meditate, <b>repent of their own sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">relationship with Christ and God <b>directly</b>; responsible to do it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Getting it both pieces\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2122.24,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:13-14 - Adam first, then Eve","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Now it's not over yet\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:13-14</b>: Adam formed <b>first</b>, then Eve; Adam not deceived, the woman deceived, fell into transgression</li><li class=\"d0\">Why bring up <b>Adam</b>? What was Adam created to do?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>priest</b> (also king - \"those two went together\")</li><li class=\"d1\">serving in the <b>temple that was paradise</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Role toward <b>Eve</b>: bring her to God as well</li><li class=\"d1\">priest first for his own family: <b>pray for, intercede for, teach</b> her about God</li><li class=\"d1\">formed first, around first → God revealed himself to him first</li><li class=\"d0\">What happened: \"the <b>exact opposite</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 3</b>: Eve converses with the <b>serpent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">eats, then offers fruit to husband → he was <b>standing right there</b> the whole time</li><li class=\"d1\">priest should <b>protect the sanctity</b>, drive the devil out</li><li class=\"d1\">correct him: \"God did say we're not to eat of this tree\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"sitting there and watching her do it and seeing what happens\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2275.39,"lv":3,"h":"Not 'women were no good from the beginning'","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>not</b> saying \"women were no good from the beginning\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Saying: if <b>men fail</b> in their role - the one at the <b>start of this passage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">women then lack <b>their</b> role: learning, coming to know God, being <b>protected</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"whole thing breaks down when you <b>invert</b> the way things are supposed to be\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>both fail</b>, both cast out - \"that's bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Marker</b>: Paul <b>not defending conventional Roman or Jewish hierarchies</b> in 1 Timothy</li><li class=\"d1\">will become clear as we go on</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Number one</b>: women equally responsible to know Christ, repent, <b>work out their salvation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">change from <b>Judaism</b>; huge difference from <b>paganism</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Number two</b>: Ephesian pagan reality = same <b>demonic inversion</b> as Genesis 3</li><li class=\"d1\">propped up a <b>feminine form</b>, adorned as a <b>goddess</b>, worshiped → destruction, sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Men fulfill role not as the <b>boss</b> or authority figure (cf. title <b>Father</b>) but as <b>priests, teachers</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Women take a <b>huge new responsibility</b>: <b>learn rather than teach</b>, know Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2433.14,"lv":2,"h":"1 Timothy 2:15 - Saved in childbearing?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One more verse - \"<b>badly translated</b> in the New King James\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:15</b> NKJV: \"she will be saved in <b>childbearing</b> if <b>they</b> continue in faith, love, holiness with self-control\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems:</li><li class=\"d1\">shifts <b>singular to plural</b> - \"who's they? Her and the kid? Her and her husband?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't make any sense. It's a bad translation\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"faith\" = <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Actually: \"<b>for</b>\" / because - <b>woman</b> will be saved by <b>childbearing</b> if <b>they</b> continue</li><li class=\"d1\">\"woman\" <b>singular</b> (why they throw in \"she\")</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they\" = <b>actual women</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2512.07,"lv":3,"h":"Greek: <i>gynē</i> vs woman writ large","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek: <i>anthropos</i> = man as <b>humanity, mankind</b>; <i>anēr</i> = <b>a man</b>, \"this guy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for women</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>gynē</i> = a woman or wife - root of <b>gynecologist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the other word = <b>womanhood</b>, woman as such</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>Eve's original name</b> - <b>Isha</b>, woman</li><li class=\"d0\">So: <b>Eve</b>, the concept of womanhood, \"<b>woman writ large</b>\" - as Adam's name means man</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Woman is saved by childbearing if they continue\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two subjects</b> of <b>two verbs</b> - important</li><li class=\"d1\">some read: \"women get saved by having babies\" - not that</li></ul>"},{"t":2600.74,"lv":3,"h":"Theotokos, the second Eve","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Woman writ large</b> saved through childbearing</li><li class=\"d1\">recall first Eve: inverted, went bad</li><li class=\"d1\">\"The woman gave birth to a child\" - as a Christian, think <b>Theotokos</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>tokos</i> = <b>childbearing</b>, bearing a child</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Irenaeus</b>, mid-2nd c.: <b>Theotokos = second Eve</b>, second woman</li><li class=\"d0\">Other places too: <b>Jeremiah</b> - \"I tell you a mystery, now the <b>woman shall encompass the man</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">woman came out of man with Eve; now woman encompasses man = <b>Christ in her womb</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = <b>second Adam</b> (Romans 5)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Eve not the last word on womanhood</b> anymore - the <b>Theotokos</b> is</li><li class=\"d1\">the childbearing = <b>birth of Christ</b>; one specific childbearing</li><li class=\"d1\">womanhood \"transformed by childbearing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First Eve vs second Eve: Eden = <b>inversion</b>; Theotokos and Christ = <b>inversion of that inversion</b> → puts things right</li></ul>"},{"t":2732.62,"lv":3,"h":"'If they continue': women saved in Christ","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second verb \"if they continue\" = <b>plural</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now about <b>women</b>, not woman writ large</li><li class=\"d0\">Women saved if they continue in <b>faithfulness, love, holiness, self-control</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why does Paul point this out?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ben Sirach</b>: similar argument, <b>different conclusion</b> - \"kind of gross\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Eve sinned first → \"<b>women are evil from the beginning</b>\" - explicitly</li><li class=\"d1\">not Paul quoting Sirach directly</li><li class=\"d1\">but Sirach = a strand of <b>Pharisaic tradition</b> Paul would have heard</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul points to <b>Theotokos</b> to correct that</li><li class=\"d1\">Eve isn't the last word</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing <b>evil</b> or <b>defective</b> about women - \"you pagans\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>womanhood itself redeemed</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Transformation</b> of man-woman relationship in Theotokos and Christ → salvation <b>available to women</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why they must <b>learn and develop a relationship personally</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2849.98,"lv":2,"h":"Men and women: parallel paths to salvation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not just \"men doing better than Adam\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ did better than Adam</b>: second Adam, completes what Adam failed</li><li class=\"d1\">men, <b>in emulation of Christ</b>, find salvation in part by the <b>priestly role</b> Adam failed</li><li class=\"d0\">Women find salvation <b>in Christ</b> - Theotokos transformed the relationship</li><li class=\"d1\">by continuing in <b>faithfulness, love, holiness, self-control</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"same things he's always telling men to have\" - <b>in like manner</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not a completely different thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">expressed through <b>two different parallel roles</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2917.87,"lv":2,"h":"Closing: Reddit warning and next time","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not endorsing <b>traditional hierarchies</b> - \"that's where people are gonna <b>flip out on Reddit</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"they'll see what I'm talking about when we go on\"</li><li class=\"d1\">will be talking about <b>slaves</b>, for example</li><li class=\"d0\">Pick up at <b>beginning of chapter 3</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next time: beginning of <b>1 Timothy 3</b>"});
